A||||3||geyser||rear control arm "bearings"||||1||
Z||000000||geyser||12-22-1999||03:04 AM||||Anyone have any experience using the rear control arm bearings (Herb Adams) offered by Moroso? 84-87 Fiero, Moroso part # 87045.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||12-22-1999||07:39 PM||||I haven't seen how they are built, but I imagine Moroso designed them mostly for racing. I expect they may not last long on a street car without constant care. Some people use spherical joints welded into the eyes on the arm. Those are also known to not last unless they are taken good care of.||||reg||1
Z||000002||geyser||12-22-1999||10:35 PM||||Ogre, there are 7 pcs to each bearing, and a grease fitting on the shell, look very racy. Moroso also lists a rear sway bar at 1.25" dia. and a front at 1.00" dia. The parts are from Herb Adams racing and distributed by Moroso.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||12-23-1999||09:37 PM||||sway bars that heavy on the car could actually cause problems in turns and braking especially on the road. They could make the car so ridged that wheels leave the ground on small bumps they would normally follow.<P>You'd need to keep the bearings greased for sure. Absolutely at every oilchange.<P>How well they will tolerate road pounding I don't know. They are really race parts. Race parts tend to not always be good on the road. (AL connecting rods in the motor come to mind. These don't always do well in daily driving.)||||reg||1
